  The loading question is less obvious than it looks. [38;5;153mindex.html[39m uses a classic [38;5;153m<script src="app.js">[39m, and [38;5;153mapp.js[39m has no
  imports or exports — so there's currently no module system on the browser side, while [38;5;153msrc/[39m is CommonJS.

  - [1mES modules[0m — [38;5;153mindex.html[39m becomes [38;5;153m<script type="module" src="app.js">[39m and [38;5;153mapp.js[39m imports the store. Native, no build
    step, real imports, and Node's test runner can import the same files directly. The catch: ES modules are fetched
    under CORS rules, so opening [38;5;153mindex.html[39m by double-clicking it ([38;5;153mfile://[39m) stops working — you'd need any static server
    ([38;5;153mnpx serve .[39m, [38;5;153mpython3 -m http.server[39m).
  - [1mClassic scripts with a global[0m — add [38;5;153m<script src="src/prefs/store.js">[39m before [38;5;153mapp.js[39m and hang [38;5;153mwindow.Preferences[39m off
    it. Keeps [38;5;153mfile://[39m working. Costs: a global, an implicit load-order dependency, and test setup needs shims because
    Node can't cleanly import a file written for the global pattern.

  One wrinkle with ES modules: [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m has no [38;5;153m"type"[39m field, so Node treats [38;5;153m.js[39m as CommonJS and would choke on
  [38;5;153mexport[39m syntax. Adding [38;5;153m"type": "module"[39m at the top level would break the existing [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m and [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m, which
  I don't want to touch. The contained fix is a one-line [38;5;153msrc/prefs/package.json[39m containing [38;5;153m{"type": "module"}[39m, which
  scopes ESM to that directory and leaves the rest of the repo alone. It's a slightly obscure Node idiom, but it beats
  renaming files to [38;5;153m.mjs[39m (some static servers don't serve [38;5;153m.mjs[39m with a JavaScript MIME type, which breaks module loading
  in the browser) and it beats touching unrelated files.

  [1mI recommend ES modules with the scoped [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m.[0m The [38;5;153mfile://[39m loss is real but cheap to work around, and the
  alternative pushes cost into every test you write.

  The second thing I want to settle: you chose a generic store with no fixed schema, which means nothing in the app
  reads or writes a preference yet. That's a coherent choice, but it makes "settings persist across sessions" impossible
  to verify by using the app — only by tests. One small real consumer would close that loop.
